imagination can only take you so far so  what I'm going to talk about here I had  actually considered doing this  specifically as a Doctor Who  centered video because these are  thoughts that I've had about Chris  Chibnall being the showrunner for a  female doctor and I thought about just  focusing in on that but I kind of want  to talk about it in broader strokes in  general on a more philosophical level  than just sort of hammering in on the on  this one thing and I want to talk about  when writers write outside of their  experience and the reason my initial  thoughts were to talk Doctor Who is  because we obviously now have a female  doctor and I found myself wondering you  know the idea of a female doctor was one  that I was initially it's very skeptical  of and I'm you know I'm not worried  about that inherently anymore I mean I  still have my worries about a new series  because I would be worried about any new  actor with a new field runner coming in  so but I did find myself wondering when  the time came to do a female doctor  would it have been a better idea to have  a female showrunner a female head writer  because when people write outside of  their own core experience things can  sometimes go awry now before I get into  some examples of what I'm talking about  let me begin by saying that I am NOT  laying out some kind of thing where like  you can only write what you know if you  don't if you don't have a first-hand  experience you can't write about it I  would never tell any writer of any  format whether it be television books  comic books movies whatever that they  can't write a certain kind of character  of course you can anybody can take  crack at writing anything I do believe  that but there are certain pitfalls and  certain traps that I see come up  repeatedly  when people attempt to write a character  that is distinctly unlike themselves in  their experience so obviously the idea  of male of men writing female characters  and you know white people write black  people and you know cisgender people  writing trans characters anything like  that and again this is not a case of  like every time somebody steps outside  their experience they get it wrong no  there are ton of instances of you know  men writing women extremely well of  white people writing black characters  from you all black writers writing white  characters extremely well black writers  writing white characters extremely well  but going through these is making my  tongue just read out fail me but you get  what I'm saying like there are tons of  businesses of this working so don't act  me all the times that this works I just  want to talk about the pitfalls that  happen and that I sometime and when I  try to point these out I do sometimes  get pushback of like oh are you saying I  can't write it no write whatever you  want but when something is outside of  your experience that should be done with  a little bit more care and ideally a  little bit more research so some of the  things that come to mind the podcasts  that I do with my partner Liz now called  tough like a girl we had to rename it  for a very stupid reason but we covered  Thor goddess of thunder the first trade  collected trade volume of that and it  was good actually it's quite good but  there is something that happens and I  think the actual issue had happened it  was five it was four or five I think it  was five we're the new Thor who's now a  woman confronts the absorbing man and  titania and it's an okay action beat  but there are bits of it that are really  really cringey because well for a couple  things the the writer decided to turn  absorbent man into a straw man for  people who were criticizing the idea of  a female Thor in the first place which  is a bit of a cheap go at people but  even as that goes on you know there's  all this emphasis that you know that  this villain is putting on oh you know  why'd you have to take a guy's title  Thor was his name like what the heck and  th