ch  as I love a lot of these things that  I've seen it turn up in that girl I've  seen it turn up in Buffy stuff where you  know there's this really over emphasis  on girl power not just having it like  females with strength but like labeling  it girl power  having it pointed out by characters man  women are tough I almost never see that  when a woman writes these characters  because they'll just write them as tough  and let it stand whereas I think you  know and I've mentioned this before but  I mean I have aspirations as a fiction  writer so so I understand you know the  insecurities that you can get in trying  to write characters you know you want to  be sure that everybody gets what it is  you're doing that people see the effort  you put into it but highlighting it like  that just makes it weird and awkward and  cringy because like to contrast  something like that for  scene with say Ms marvel Kamala Khan I  love her book so much and what's and I  think part of the reason that it is  written as accessible as it is and that  these characters come off as genuine as  they do is because the writer G willow  Wilson is herself a Muslim and she's  writing Muslim characters now again is  that a my does that mean that a  Christian writer couldn't write them  just as well or an atheist or anything  no absolutely not but the thing is that  she was able to draw directly from her  own well of knowledge and of experience  and present these characters in a  fully-formed way whereas someone who has  to you know either yeah if they're good  about it do a lot of research or if  they're lazy about it make a heck of a  lot of presumptions they would they  would be pouring all these facts they'd  be CRO barring in these things and not  necessarily knowing how they actually  you know because you can do research but  it's hard to know how the facts of  something like living a Muslim life and  growing up in a Muslim family how those  actually play out in a living situation  I mean you 